    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d128">

                <pb facs="0229" n="127" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg127"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/506: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-10-16T17:00:00-05:00">October 16, 1935—5
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>303. Your 520, October 16, noon.<anchor n="77" xml:id="d128-f1"/> Following from
                    Navy:</p>
                <p>Sentence beginning “we see the logic”<anchor n="78" xml:id="d128-f2"/> refers to
                    paragraph 4 of your 485<anchor n="79" xml:id="d128-f3"/> beginning “Regarding
                    destroyers”. We indicate in the sentence referred to that we will consider any
                    proposal British may make to end difficulty in which they find themselves in
                    respect to qualitative limitations of destroyers, suggesting in the same
                    sentence a general method of solution by transfer between destroyer and cruiser
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) categories.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
